1
00:00:08,500 --> 00:00:09,766
[car starting]

2
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:12,733
[car revving]

3
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:14,533
[jazz music playing]

4
00:00:18,918 --> 00:00:22,122
[Jerry] This is an eyebrow-frizzing,
retina-melting,

5
00:00:22,188 --> 00:00:28,294
absurdly gorgeous
1969 Lamborghini P400S Miura.

6
00:00:29,299 --> 00:00:34,033
Just 43 inches tall,
it is painted in Arancio Borealis,

7
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:36,533
-Italian for "orange."
-[engine revs]

8
00:00:36,600 --> 00:00:40,699
It has a mid-engine 360-horsepower V12
mounted sideways.

9
00:00:41,266 --> 00:00:43,699
And it's got eyelashes on its headlights.

10
00:00:44,199 --> 00:00:45,166
Jesus!

11
00:00:45,433 --> 00:00:49,500
It's incredibly masculine
and incredibly feminine at the same time.

12
00:00:50,099 --> 00:00:53,633
It is, in my opinion,
the most beautiful car ever designed.

13
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:55,300
[car accelerates]

14
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:57,066
[phone ringing]

15
00:00:57,366 --> 00:00:58,300
[Chris] Hello.

16
00:00:58,366 --> 00:00:59,500
[Jerry] Hey, Rock.

17
00:00:59,766 --> 00:01:00,666
What are you doing?

18
00:01:00,800 --> 00:01:02,500
[Chris] I'm not doing much.

19
00:01:02,796 --> 00:01:03,730
Outside my house.

20
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:05,233
[Jerry] It's nice out.

21
00:01:05,366 --> 00:01:07,033
[Chris] It's amazing. It's beautiful.

22
00:01:07,333 --> 00:01:08,633
[Jerry] How about some coffee?

23
00:01:08,833 --> 00:01:09,800
[Chris] Let's do it.

24
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:16,399
[Jerry] Hi, I'm Jerry Seinfeld.

25
00:01:16,500 --> 00:01:19,633
And this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

26
00:01:20,966 --> 00:01:24,866
My guest today is my comedy buddy
and always brilliant, Chris Rock.

27
00:01:26,866 --> 00:01:27,800
[Chris] SeinTime.

28
00:01:27,854 --> 00:01:28,922
[Jerry] So this is it.

29
00:01:29,089 --> 00:01:30,890
[Chris] This is my Tony Soprano house.

30
00:01:31,333 --> 00:01:32,500
[Jerry] You're a made guy.

31
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:33,533
Where's Carm?

32
00:01:36,966 --> 00:01:37,800
[Chris] Wow!

33
00:01:37,933 --> 00:01:40,600
-What is this?
-It's the most beautiful car in the world.

34
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:41,966
[engine revving]

35
00:01:42,633 --> 00:01:43,800
All right, here we go.

36
00:01:50,733 --> 00:01:52,133
Is this thing gonna take off?

37
00:01:52,345 --> 00:01:53,313
Yes, it is.

38
00:01:57,399 --> 00:01:59,800
[Jerry] When this car
came on the scene in '66,

39
00:01:59,899 --> 00:02:02,066
it was so shocking and so different,

40
00:02:02,133 --> 00:02:04,366
it blew everybody else away.

41
00:02:04,733 --> 00:02:06,033
So it reminds me of you.

42
00:02:06,199 --> 00:02:08,633
[Chris] So it's a screaming person,
black car.

43
00:02:08,733 --> 00:02:09,699
[both laughing]

44
00:02:09,833 --> 00:02:11,033
[Jerry] It is. It screams.

45
00:02:11,164 --> 00:02:12,966
[car speeds up]

46
00:02:14,933 --> 00:02:15,966
How do you like the car?

47
00:02:16,066 --> 00:02:17,766
-I like you, Jerry.
-Thank you, Chris.

48
00:02:17,833 --> 00:02:19,900
And I say everything's about company.

49
00:02:20,033 --> 00:02:22,266
A gourmet meal with an asshole...

50
00:02:22,366 --> 00:02:24,100
-[Jerry laughing]
-...is a horrible meal.

51
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:27,166
A hotdog with an interesting person...

52
00:02:27,299 --> 00:02:30,533
-Is a great meal. It's an amazing meal.
-...is an amazing meal.

53
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:33,786
[Chris] It's all about the company.
If we were in a cab,

54
00:02:34,066 --> 00:02:37,166
we'd probably be having
the same, exact conversation.

55
00:02:40,466 --> 00:02:42,900
[Chris] I remember your assistant
took your car that time.

56
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,866
[Jerry] To get his cousin out of jail.

57
00:02:45,131 --> 00:02:47,167
-Whatever, he just took your car.
-And parked it...

58
00:02:47,233 --> 00:02:50,133
Don't still buy his story ten years later.

59
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:51,066
Well...

60
00:02:51,133 --> 00:02:53,733
'Cause there's no other way
to get your cousin out of jail.

61
00:02:53,799 --> 00:02:54,633
[Jerry chuckles]

62
00:02:54,700 --> 00:02:56,566
Nope, you got to do five more years

63
00:02:56,766 --> 00:02:59,533
'cause someone had the nerve
to pick you up in a cheap car.

64
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:03,700
Here's my good jail story.

65
00:03:03,766 --> 00:03:07,400
My brother was in the halfway house,
in between jail and whatever.

66
00:03:07,533 --> 00:03:10,566
So, one day I'm dropping him off at jail,
and then I realize,

67
00:03:10,933 --> 00:03:13,600
I'm waiting for him
to safely get into jail.

68
00:03:13,666 --> 00:03:15,033
[laughing]

69
00:03:15,333 --> 00:03:17,966
God forbid something happened to him
out here.

70
00:03:18,066 --> 00:03:19,600
You know what, you're a good brother.

71
00:03:19,666 --> 00:03:20,700
I'm a good brother.

72
00:03:25,666 --> 00:03:30,299
People ask me for $5000 like it's asking,
"When's the next bus coming?"

73
00:03:30,366 --> 00:03:31,466
[laughing]

74
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:34,133
"Hey, man, you got $5000?"

75
00:03:34,933 --> 00:03:36,733
No one sits me down.

76
00:03:37,299 --> 00:03:39,233
No one even tells me a story anymore.

77
00:03:39,299 --> 00:03:43,133
They just ask me for $5000
like it's the time.

78
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:44,033
[laughing]

79
00:03:44,100 --> 00:03:45,433
Like it's the time.

80
00:03:45,758 --> 00:03:47,860
You can always tell people
who call you up for money.

81
00:03:47,933 --> 00:03:50,266
"Hey, we need to talk."
"Aren't you talking now?"

82
00:03:50,333 --> 00:03:51,666
[both chuckle]

83
00:03:51,833 --> 00:03:54,333
"You need to corner me,
is that what you're suggesting?"

84
00:03:54,399 --> 00:03:56,133
[laughing]

85
00:03:56,970 --> 00:03:58,905
"Uh, I need to corner you later."

86
00:03:59,266 --> 00:04:00,600
[both laughing]

87
00:04:03,666 --> 00:04:05,033
Sometimes I try and figure out,

88
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:06,766
like, you take different inventions

89
00:04:06,833 --> 00:04:09,399
and you put them
in a different time in history.

90
00:04:09,533 --> 00:04:11,966
Like, if you were a caveman
and you had a lighter,

91
00:04:12,166 --> 00:04:13,566
you'd be king of the world, right?

92
00:04:13,633 --> 00:04:15,066
You'd be absolute king of the world.

93
00:04:15,133 --> 00:04:19,533
If you were in the Civil War
and you had an AK-47,

94
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:21,633
-the war's over with one guy.
-One guy.

95
00:04:22,033 --> 00:04:24,866
[Chris] When we were young comics,
we were looking for advantages.

96
00:04:25,066 --> 00:04:26,233
Cheats, steroids.

97
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:29,333
Would I take a pill to be able to write
and direct a movie like Woody Allen?

98
00:04:29,369 --> 00:04:31,170
You're goddamn right I would.

99
00:04:31,666 --> 00:04:33,966
-[growls]
-That's a horrible analogy.

100
00:04:34,633 --> 00:04:35,866
[Chris] Steroids, I don't know.

101
00:04:36,033 --> 00:04:40,633
It's just an advantage to make more money,
like any other business.

102
00:04:40,833 --> 00:04:42,433
Every stockbroker had a cell phone,

103
00:04:42,500 --> 00:04:44,900
and then one day
one guy had the BlackBerry.

104
00:04:45,099 --> 00:04:47,000
And for about three months,

105
00:04:47,066 --> 00:04:49,733
he had a humongous advantage
over everybody.

106
00:04:49,966 --> 00:04:52,766
BlackBerries don't turn you
into Lyle Alzado.

107
00:04:52,959 --> 00:04:55,895
Getting information quicker,
getting to the quarterback quicker.

108
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:57,366
Tell me the difference.

109
00:04:57,666 --> 00:04:59,599
So, really, steroids don't bother you?

110
00:04:59,666 --> 00:05:00,766
-Ridiculous.
-They bother me

111
00:05:00,833 --> 00:05:02,433
because they influence kids.

112
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:03,766
They are kids.

113
00:05:04,066 --> 00:05:05,833
These players are kids.

114
00:05:06,066 --> 00:05:08,700
Hey, there's rules.
These guys broke the rules.

115
00:05:09,066 --> 00:05:10,200
They should be punished.

116
00:05:10,366 --> 00:05:11,500
There's math,

117
00:05:12,166 --> 00:05:14,033
and then everything else is debatable.

118
00:05:14,099 --> 00:05:14,933
[chuckles softly]

119
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:19,066
And now we are entering Jersey
where they pump gas for you.

120
00:05:19,519 --> 00:05:21,654
-There's no self service in Jersey.
-Oh, really? Why?

121
00:05:22,133 --> 00:05:23,533
Chris Christie, I guess.

122
00:05:24,599 --> 00:05:28,500
I can't believe how close to the name
Chris Christie is to Krispy Kreme.

123
00:05:32,200 --> 00:05:33,333
Have you eaten yet today?

124
00:05:33,566 --> 00:05:35,433
[Chris] I've eaten some fruit today.

125
00:05:35,599 --> 00:05:37,266
I'm doing a movie in a couple of months.

126
00:05:37,537 --> 00:05:40,340
-But I'll actually eat a burger.
-[Jerry] You had KFC three nights ago.

127
00:05:40,433 --> 00:05:42,833
So you cheat.
You gonna throw me out the Hall of Fame?

128
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:44,633
'Cause I had Kentucky Fried Chicken?

129
00:05:46,900 --> 00:05:48,566
Two-piece with a biscuit

130
00:05:48,633 --> 00:05:52,166
is one of the greatest meals
ever invented.

131
00:05:52,333 --> 00:05:53,633
Have you ever had Popeyes?

132
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:54,966
Popeyes is too good.

133
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:56,200
After you have Popeyes,

134
00:05:56,266 --> 00:06:00,666
you need one of those Men in Black
memory sticks to erase your memory,

135
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:02,428
or else you will come back to this place

136
00:06:02,495 --> 00:06:03,529
-every day.
-Every day.

137
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:04,731
[train horn blows]

138
00:06:08,900 --> 00:06:11,366
[Chris] I think Lola had a basketball game
out here this year.

139
00:06:11,433 --> 00:06:14,533
My oldest daughter,
Lola, plays basketball.

140
00:06:16,542 --> 00:06:18,478
[inaudible dialogue]

141
00:06:28,133 --> 00:06:29,200
[indistinct chatter]

142
00:06:32,658 --> 00:06:34,961
-How you doing?
-Good. Can I get you something to drink?

143
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:36,733
I would like to start with some coffee.

144
00:06:36,866 --> 00:06:38,599
-I'll take coffee with cream.
-You got it.

145
00:06:43,666 --> 00:06:46,299
[Chris] 10:00 p.m. Tonight Show,
that's Conan's fault.

146
00:06:46,433 --> 00:06:48,433
Know what the problem is?
Conan's not a comedian.

147
00:06:48,574 --> 00:06:49,809
-He's a talk-show host.
-Right.

148
00:06:50,042 --> 00:06:52,745
And any comedian
would have spotted that a mile away.

149
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:54,933
-Right.
-'Cause we're used to getting booked.

150
00:06:55,033 --> 00:06:58,700
We know what the day before,
the day after, we know what that means.

151
00:06:58,751 --> 00:07:00,319
It's true, you would smell that, you go,

152
00:07:00,386 --> 00:07:02,655
"What?
There's gonna be an hour of talk show?"

153
00:07:02,733 --> 00:07:05,333
-"In front of mine. On a one-way street."
-[chuckles]

154
00:07:05,933 --> 00:07:09,200
It's like, "I'm dating Beyoncé
and Alicia Keys." No, you're not.

155
00:07:09,766 --> 00:07:12,266
You're gonna date one of them
or you're gonna lose both of them.

156
00:07:12,533 --> 00:07:14,900
As my brother would say, "Pick a bitch."

157
00:07:15,133 --> 00:07:17,200
The analogy always
has to be black artists, right?

158
00:07:17,266 --> 00:07:21,066
Yeah, I'm black. Jerry, I'm trying to...

159
00:07:21,633 --> 00:07:24,400
Hey, sugar's not a bad thing.

160
00:07:25,244 --> 00:07:26,746
-Want sugar?
-Okay, the real sugar.

161
00:07:26,833 --> 00:07:28,299
I'm looking at this thing like...

162
00:07:31,833 --> 00:07:33,466
I'm gonna get the chicken salad.

163
00:07:33,599 --> 00:07:37,200
It's probably the worst thing
you could eat on camera is chicken salad.

164
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:39,866
-Get some fries?
-Yeah, a basket of fries?

165
00:07:40,200 --> 00:07:41,433
You all right. I like you.

166
00:07:41,866 --> 00:07:43,000
Who do you envy?

167
00:07:43,166 --> 00:07:45,633
I admire people that have breakdowns.

168
00:07:45,733 --> 00:07:47,166
-You admire them?
-Yeah.

169
00:07:47,233 --> 00:07:50,503
'Cause once you have a breakdown, then you
can clear the slate, and you're like...

170
00:07:50,666 --> 00:07:52,433
Everything you do is, like...

171
00:07:52,599 --> 00:07:53,833
Like Eliot Spitzer?

172
00:07:53,966 --> 00:07:55,733
-If he really did something now...
-Yeah.

173
00:07:56,099 --> 00:07:57,799
-Wow. Look at that.
-Yeah.

174
00:08:00,112 --> 00:08:01,280
Tiger Woods is back.

175
00:08:01,347 --> 00:08:02,515
He's got a new girl.

176
00:08:02,833 --> 00:08:04,766
And he's a guy that needs a relationship.

177
00:08:04,884 --> 00:08:08,120
He's a guy that needs
to make that call every night.

178
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:10,533
"All right. I'm in."

179
00:08:10,590 --> 00:08:12,058
-"Had a good round."
-"It was good."

180
00:08:12,258 --> 00:08:14,193
I remember, years ago,
I guess Prince was married.

181
00:08:14,266 --> 00:08:15,566
I was hanging out with Prince.

182
00:08:15,766 --> 00:08:17,799
And he was like,
"Man, I gotta go make that call."

183
00:08:19,033 --> 00:08:22,766
Prince, calling his wife,
"Yeah, okay. Yeah."

184
00:08:22,933 --> 00:08:28,066
He made the most routine call
to his wife you could ever see.

185
00:08:28,166 --> 00:08:30,533
That's all the marriage counseling
you really need.

186
00:08:30,599 --> 00:08:33,333
Prince knew, "I gotta make this call."

187
00:08:37,766 --> 00:08:40,566
[Jerry] My favorite thing is still
the thing you did to me.

188
00:08:40,833 --> 00:08:43,133
-What was Oprah's play? The...
-The Color Purple?

189
00:08:43,199 --> 00:08:44,066
The Color Purple.

190
00:08:44,133 --> 00:08:46,199
And we were at the after-party
at the Four Seasons.

191
00:08:46,266 --> 00:08:49,433
I was standing around, and I was
with Jess, we were schmoozing.

192
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:51,466
We locked eyes. You came over to me.
Remember this?

193
00:08:51,533 --> 00:08:53,733
You grabbed my arms
and you went, "Comedian."

194
00:08:56,766 --> 00:08:59,433
-I needed a comedian.
-I flash on that moment all the time.

195
00:08:59,833 --> 00:09:01,800
Somebody was saying the other day about...

196
00:09:01,866 --> 00:09:04,433
"Gotta be funny or they'll turn on you,"
a regular person told me.

197
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:05,466
"They will turn on you."

198
00:09:05,566 --> 00:09:08,000
I go, "They should turn on you."

199
00:09:08,066 --> 00:09:10,400
It's a very humbling life at any level.

200
00:09:10,466 --> 00:09:11,666
-You can't cheat them.
-No.

201
00:09:11,966 --> 00:09:16,400
The ability to talk
to a lot of people is freakish.

202
00:09:16,533 --> 00:09:21,066
It's more freakish than being
able to run fast or dunk a basketball

203
00:09:21,133 --> 00:09:23,166
or any of those other things.

204
00:09:23,533 --> 00:09:25,000
It's freakish.

205
00:09:25,300 --> 00:09:28,199
You think Superman could talk
to 1000 people at one time?

206
00:09:28,266 --> 00:09:29,900
-[chuckling]
-Superman could, yeah.

207
00:09:29,966 --> 00:09:32,766
He can get their attention,
'cause he has to bend something first.

208
00:09:33,166 --> 00:09:36,800
But to just get up in front of 1000 people
and start talking?

209
00:09:36,866 --> 00:09:39,633
They would give him a few minutes,
and then if he's not funny...

210
00:09:39,699 --> 00:09:43,233
Yeah and every time he would lose,
he'd have to break something.

211
00:09:43,282 --> 00:09:44,650
He'd lose the room. Eventually...

212
00:09:44,933 --> 00:09:47,300
Or burn something
with his X-ray vision or something.

213
00:09:47,366 --> 00:09:50,033
He'd fly, come back.
"Okay. I got your attention."

214
00:09:50,433 --> 00:09:54,500
Any place I go, if there's a microphone,
people want me to speak.

215
00:09:54,733 --> 00:09:59,366
A graduation, a book club thing,
a book signing...

216
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:01,500
Wherever there's a microphone,

217
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:04,433
someone's gonna come up to me and go...

218
00:10:04,666 --> 00:10:06,800
-Yeah, we can...
-I can go to a stranger's funeral...

219
00:10:07,199 --> 00:10:08,533
Have you ever talked at a funeral?

220
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:10,233
I talked at a friend's funeral this year.

221
00:10:10,933 --> 00:10:11,766
Yeah.

222
00:10:11,900 --> 00:10:12,933
Got a couple laughs.

223
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:13,833
[laughs]

224
00:10:18,800 --> 00:10:22,466
The great thing about you,
there's no subject you cannot handle.

225
00:10:22,521 --> 00:10:24,824
You have given some thought
to almost everything.

226
00:10:25,333 --> 00:10:27,766
-We're professional arguers.
-Yeah.

227
00:10:27,833 --> 00:10:32,100
Not only can we argue about anything,
we can argue either side.

228
00:10:33,366 --> 00:10:35,166
I'm working on a bit about bullying.

229
00:10:35,234 --> 00:10:37,370
Everybody's trying to get rid of bullying.
I'm like, "You crazy?"

230
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:38,800
Some of these kids need this.

231
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:40,966
I'll go even further.
Most of them need it.

232
00:10:41,433 --> 00:10:42,533
Who's gonna cure AIDS?

233
00:10:43,033 --> 00:10:44,699
Who's gonna invent the fuel

234
00:10:44,744 --> 00:10:47,013
-that gets us off of fossil fuel?
-That's right.

235
00:10:47,266 --> 00:10:48,666
-Who's gonna do these things?
-Yeah.

236
00:10:48,933 --> 00:10:51,133
Some guy that was bullied,
that's who's gonna do it.

237
00:10:51,466 --> 00:10:55,266
I know. My wife is always trying to stop
the boys from beating on each other.

238
00:10:55,388 --> 00:10:57,323
I'm saying,
"No, they gotta learn, this is it."

239
00:10:57,433 --> 00:10:59,300
-This is life.
-Does your wife do that?

240
00:10:59,366 --> 00:11:01,066
Trying to stop the fights all the time?

241
00:11:01,133 --> 00:11:03,600
-Wanting everything to be fair.
-You hate that.

242
00:11:03,666 --> 00:11:05,533
I hate it. Lola had a basketball game.

243
00:11:05,600 --> 00:11:07,533
Coach didn't put her in.
